<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ CATEGORY 1: NETWORKING -->
<div class="cat-bar"></div>
<section id="networking">
<div class="category-header">
  <div class="cat-label" style="color:var(--accent)">Category 01</div>
  <div class="cat-title">Networking<br><span style="color:var(--accent)">Fundamentals</span></div>
  <div class="cat-line" style="background:var(--accent)"></div>
</div>

<div class="cards-grid">

  <!-- IP ADDRESS -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🌐</span>
      <span class="card-num">#01</span>
      <div class="card-title">IP Address</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Internet Protocol Address</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">An IP address is a unique numerical label assigned to every device connected to a network. Think of it like a home address — just as the post office needs your address to deliver mail, the internet needs your IP address to send and receive data to the right device.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent)">📦 Two Versions</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">IPv4  →  192.168.1.1
         (4 numbers, 0-255, separated by dots)
         Supports ~4.3 billion addresses

IPv6  →  2001:0db8:85a3::8a2e:0370:7334
         (8 groups of hex, separated by colons)
         Supports 340 undecillion addresses 🤯</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--border)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--muted)">Public vs Private</div>
        <p>Your <strong style="color:var(--text)">Public IP</strong> is visible on the internet (given by your ISP). Your <strong style="color:var(--text)">Private IP</strong> is only used inside your home/office network (like 192.168.x.x).</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(0,229,255,.1);color:var(--accent)">IPv4</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(0,229,255,.1);color:var(--accent)">IPv6</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(107,132,168,.1);color:var(--muted)">Addressing</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- MAC ADDRESS -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent3)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🔌</span>
      <span class="card-num">#02</span>
      <div class="card-title">MAC Address</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Media Access Control Address</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">A MAC address is a unique hardware identifier permanently burned into every network interface card (NIC) by the manufacturer. Unlike an IP address, it never changes — it's like a serial number tattooed onto your network card at birth.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent3)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent3)">🔍 Format</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">  A4 : C3 : F0 : 85 : AC : 2B
  └─────┘   └──────────────┘
  OUI            Device ID
  (Manufacturer) (Unique per device)

  OUI = Organizationally Unique Identifier
  Identifies who MADE the network card</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-info">
        <span class="alert-icon">💡</span>
        <span>MAC addresses work at <strong>Layer 2</strong> (Data Link) of the OSI model. They're used within a local network, while IP addresses handle routing across the internet.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.1);color:var(--accent3)">Hardware</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.1);color:var(--accent3)">Layer 2</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(107,132,168,.1);color:var(--muted)">Permanent ID</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ARP -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent4)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🔗</span>
      <span class="card-num">#03</span>
      <div class="card-title">ARP</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Address Resolution Protocol</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">ARP is the protocol that bridges the gap between IP addresses and MAC addresses. When your computer wants to send data to another device on the same network, it knows the IP address — but needs to find the MAC address. ARP does this translation.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent4)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent4)">⚙️ How ARP Works</div>
        <div class="steps">
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(0,255,136,.15);color:var(--accent4)">1</div>
            <div class="step-content"><strong>Your PC broadcasts:</strong> "Who has IP 192.168.1.5? Tell me your MAC!"</div>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(0,255,136,.15);color:var(--accent4)">2</div>
            <div class="step-content"><strong>Target device replies:</strong> "That's me! My MAC is AA:BB:CC:DD:EE:FF"</div>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(0,255,136,.15);color:var(--accent4)">3</div>
            <div class="step-content"><strong>Your PC saves this</strong> in its ARP cache for future use</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-danger">
        <span class="alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <span><strong>ARP Spoofing:</strong> Attackers can send fake ARP replies to redirect network traffic to their machine — a common Man-in-the-Middle attack.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(0,255,136,.1);color:var(--accent4)">Protocol</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(0,255,136,.1);color:var(--accent4)">IP↔MAC</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.1);color:var(--accent2)">Spoofable</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- DNS -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--warning)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">📖</span>
      <span class="card-num">#04</span>
      <div class="card-title">DNS</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Domain Name System</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">DNS is the internet's phonebook. Humans remember names (google.com), but computers communicate using numbers (142.250.80.46). DNS translates human-friendly domain names into machine-readable IP addresses automatically every time you browse the web.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--warning)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--warning)">🔄 DNS Resolution Steps</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">You type: www.google.com
        ↓
[Browser Cache] → Found? Done ✓
        ↓ Not found
[OS/Router Cache] → Found? Done ✓
        ↓ Not found
[Recursive Resolver] (your ISP)
        ↓
[Root Name Server] → "Ask .com servers"
        ↓
[TLD Server .com] → "Ask google's server"
        ↓
[Google's DNS Server] → 142.250.80.46 ✓</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-warn">
        <span class="alert-icon">🎯</span>
        <span><strong>DNS Poisoning/Spoofing:</strong> Attackers inject fake DNS records, redirecting users to malicious sites that look legitimate.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(255,176,32,.1);color:var(--warning)">Port 53</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(255,176,32,.1);color:var(--warning)">Name Resolution</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(107,132,168,.1);color:var(--muted)">Critical Infrastructure</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- DHCP -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent2)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">📡</span>
      <span class="card-num">#05</span>
      <div class="card-title">DHCP</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">DHCP automatically assigns IP addresses to devices joining a network, so you don't have to configure each device manually. When your phone connects to WiFi, DHCP instantly hands it an IP address, subnet mask, default gateway, and DNS server.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent2)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent2)">📬 The DORA Process</div>
        <div class="ascii-art"> Your Device          DHCP Server (Router)
     │                        │
     │──── DISCOVER ─────────▶│  "Anyone have an IP for me?"
     │                        │
     │◀─── OFFER ─────────────│  "Here, take 192.168.1.10!"
     │                        │
     │──── REQUEST ───────────▶│  "Yes please, I want that IP"
     │                        │
     │◀─── ACK ───────────────│  "Confirmed! Lease = 24hrs"</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-danger">
        <span class="alert-icon">💀</span>
        <span><strong>DHCP Starvation Attack:</strong> An attacker floods a DHCP server with fake requests, exhausting all available IPs, then sets up a rogue DHCP server.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.1);color:var(--accent2)">Auto-Config</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.1);color:var(--accent2)">Port 67/68</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(107,132,168,.1);color:var(--muted)">UDP Protocol</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</div><!-- end cards-grid -->
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ CATEGORY 2: NETWORK PROTECTION -->
<div class="cat-bar" style="background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--accent3),transparent)"></div>
<section id="protection">
<div class="category-header">
  <div class="cat-label" style="color:var(--accent3)">Category 02</div>
  <div class="cat-title">Network<br><span style="color:var(--accent3)">Protection</span></div>
  <div class="cat-line" style="background:var(--accent3)"></div>
</div>

<div class="cards-grid">

  <!-- VPN -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent3)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🔒</span>
      <span class="card-num">#06</span>
      <div class="card-title">VPN</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Virtual Private Network</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">A VPN creates an encrypted "tunnel" through the internet, making your traffic unreadable to outsiders. It also masks your real IP address by routing your connection through a server in another location. Think of it as sending mail in a locked, unmarked box.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent3)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent3)">🔐 VPN Tunnel Visualization</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">WITHOUT VPN:
  You ──[Your Data: VISIBLE]──▶ ISP ──▶ Website
         Anyone can read this!

WITH VPN:
  You ──[🔒ENCRYPTED🔒]──▶ VPN Server ──▶ Website
                               ↑
                    Your IP is hidden here!
                    Website sees VPN's IP</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--border)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--muted)">Common Use Cases</div>
        <p>Securing public WiFi connections · Remote work access to company networks · Bypassing geographic restrictions · Hiding browsing from ISPs</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.1);color:var(--accent3)">Encryption</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.1);color:var(--accent3)">Privacy</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.1);color:var(--accent3)">Tunneling</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- FIREWALL -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent4)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🧱</span>
      <span class="card-num">#07</span>
      <div class="card-title">Firewall</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Network Security Barrier</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">A firewall is a security system that monitors and controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predetermined rules. It acts as a gatekeeper between your trusted internal network and untrusted external networks like the internet.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent4)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent4)">🚦 How Rules Work</div>
        <div class="ascii-art"> INCOMING TRAFFIC
 ─────────────────────────
 Port 80  (HTTP)  → ALLOW ✓
 Port 443 (HTTPS) → ALLOW ✓
 Port 22  (SSH)   → BLOCK ✗ (unless whitelisted)
 Port 23  (Telnet)→ BLOCK ✗ (insecure protocol)
 Unknown  Port    → BLOCK ✗ (default deny)</div>
      </div>
      <table class="compare-table">
        <tbody><tr>
          <th style="color:var(--accent4)">Type</th>
          <th style="color:var(--accent4)">What it does</th>
        </tr>
        <tr><td>Packet Filter</td><td>Checks IP/port headers only</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Stateful</td><td>Tracks connection state</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Application (WAF)</td><td>Inspects content/payloads</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Next-Gen (NGFW)</td><td>Deep packet inspection + AI</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(0,255,136,.1);color:var(--accent4)">Rule-Based</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(0,255,136,.1);color:var(--accent4)">Packet Filter</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(0,255,136,.1);color:var(--accent4)">Gatekeeper</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ CATEGORY 3: MALWARE -->
<div class="cat-bar" style="background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--accent2),transparent)"></div>
<section id="malware">
<div class="category-header">
  <div class="cat-label" style="color:var(--accent2)">Category 03</div>
  <div class="cat-title">Malware<br><span style="color:var(--accent2)">Threats</span></div>
  <div class="cat-line" style="background:var(--accent2)"></div>
</div>

<div class="cards-grid">

  <!-- MALWARE -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent2)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">☣️</span>
      <span class="card-num">#08</span>
      <div class="card-title">Malware</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Malicious Software — The Umbrella Term</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Malware is the umbrella term for any software intentionally designed to cause damage, disrupt, steal, or gain unauthorized access to computer systems. Every type of malicious program — virus, worm, trojan, ransomware — is a form of malware.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent2)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent2)">🦠 Malware Family Tree</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">               MALWARE
              /   |   \    \    \
           Virus Worm Trojan Ransomware Spyware
            |                  |
          Infects          Encrypts
          files            your data</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-warn">
        <span class="alert-icon">📊</span>
        <span>According to cybersecurity reports, <strong>560,000+ new malware</strong> pieces are detected every day. Over 1 billion malware programs exist in total.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.1);color:var(--accent2)">Malicious</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.1);color:var(--accent2)">Umbrella Term</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- VIRUS -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid #ff6b35">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🦠</span>
      <span class="card-num">#09</span>
      <div class="card-title">Virus</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Self-Replicating Malicious Code</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">A computer virus is malicious code that attaches itself to legitimate files and spreads when those files are executed or shared. Like a biological virus, it needs a "host" to replicate. It cannot spread on its own — it requires human action (opening an infected file).</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:#ff6b35">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:#ff6b35">🔄 Infection Lifecycle</div>
        <div class="steps">
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(255,107,53,.15);color:#ff6b35">1</div>
            <div class="step-content"><strong>Dormant:</strong> Virus hides inside a file (e.g., document.exe)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(255,107,53,.15);color:#ff6b35">2</div>
            <div class="step-content"><strong>Triggered:</strong> You open the infected file — virus activates</div>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(255,107,53,.15);color:#ff6b35">3</div>
            <div class="step-content"><strong>Replicating:</strong> Virus copies itself into other files on your system</div>
          </div>
          <div class="step">
            <div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(255,107,53,.15);color:#ff6b35">4</div>
            <div class="step-content"><strong>Executing:</strong> Delivers payload — corrupts data, steals info, etc.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(255,107,53,.1);color:#ff6b35">Needs Host</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(255,107,53,.1);color:#ff6b35">Self-Replicating</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(107,132,168,.1);color:var(--muted)">Human-Triggered</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- WORM -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid #ff9500">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🪱</span>
      <span class="card-num">#10</span>
      <div class="card-title">Worm</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Self-Spreading Network Malware</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">A worm is like a virus but far more dangerous — it spreads automatically across networks WITHOUT needing human interaction. It exploits security vulnerabilities to copy itself from machine to machine, consuming bandwidth and crashing systems.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:#ff9500">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:#ff9500">🌐 Worm Spreading Pattern</div>
        <div class="ascii-art"> PC-A (infected)
  ├──▶ PC-B (infected) ──▶ PC-D
  ├──▶ PC-C (infected) ──▶ PC-E
  │                    └──▶ PC-F
  └──▶ PC-G (infected) ──▶ ...
  
  Exponential spread: 1 → 2 → 4 → 8 → 16...</div>
      </div>
      <table class="compare-table">
        <tbody><tr><th style="color:#ff9500">Feature</th><th style="color:#ff9500">Virus</th><th style="color:#ff9500">Worm</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Needs host file?</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Spreads automatically?</td><td>❌ No</td><td>✅ Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Human action needed?</td><td>✅ Yes</td><td>❌ No</td></tr>
      </tbody></table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- TROJAN -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent3)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🐴</span>
      <span class="card-num">#11</span>
      <div class="card-title">Trojan Horse</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Deceptive Malware Disguised as Legitimate Software</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Named after the ancient Greek myth, a Trojan disguises itself as legitimate, useful software. You willingly install it thinking it's a game, tool, or update — but hidden inside is malicious code. Unlike viruses/worms, Trojans don't self-replicate.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent3)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent3)">🎭 The Deception</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">What you THINK you downloaded:
  📦 "Free_Adobe_Photoshop_2025.exe"

What's ACTUALLY inside:
  📦 = [Photoshop look-alike UI]
       + [Keylogger recording passwords]
       + [Remote Access Tool for hacker]
       + [Crypto miner using your CPU]</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-danger">
        <span class="alert-icon">🚨</span>
        <span>Common Trojan types include <strong>RAT</strong> (Remote Access Trojan), <strong>Banking Trojans</strong> that steal credentials, and <strong>Downloader Trojans</strong> that install more malware.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- RANSOMWARE -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent2); grid-column: span 1">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">💰</span>
      <span class="card-num">#12</span>
      <div class="card-title">Ransomware</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Extortion via Encryption</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Ransomware encrypts your files or locks your system, then demands a ransom payment (usually in cryptocurrency) to restore access. It's one of the most financially devastating cyberattacks, costing businesses billions annually.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent2)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent2)">⚙️ Attack Flow</div>
        <div class="steps">
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.15);color:var(--accent2)">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Infection:</strong> Via phishing email, drive-by download, or exposed RDP</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.15);color:var(--accent2)">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Persistence:</strong> Establishes foothold, spreads across network</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.15);color:var(--accent2)">3</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Encryption:</strong> All .doc, .pdf, .jpg files encrypted with attacker's key</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.15);color:var(--accent2)">4</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Ransom Note:</strong> "Pay $50,000 in Bitcoin within 48 hours or lose everything"</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-warn">
        <span class="alert-icon">📊</span>
        <span>Famous examples: <strong>WannaCry (2017)</strong> infected 230,000 computers in 150 countries. <strong>Colonial Pipeline (2021)</strong> ransom was $4.4 million.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SPYWARE -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid #9b59b6">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">👁️</span>
      <span class="card-num">#13</span>
      <div class="card-title">Spyware</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Silent Surveillance Software</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Spyware secretly monitors your computer activity and sends information to a third party without your knowledge or consent. It operates silently in the background, collecting keystrokes, screenshots, browsing history, and passwords.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:#9b59b6">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:#9b59b6">🔍 What Spyware Collects</div>
        <div class="ascii-art"> Keyboard input → [Keylogger]
 Screen content → [Screengrabber]
 Passwords      → [Form grabber]
 Browser history → [Web tracker]
 Webcam feed    → [Camjacker] 😨
 Location data  → [GPS tracker]
          ↓
 All sent silently to attacker's server</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-info">
        <span class="alert-icon">💡</span>
        <span><strong>Adware</strong> is a milder cousin of spyware — it displays unwanted ads and may track browsing to target those ads. Often bundled with free software.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ CATEGORY 4: SOCIAL ENGINEERING -->
<div class="cat-bar" style="background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--warning),transparent)"></div>
<section id="social">
<div class="category-header">
  <div class="cat-label" style="color:var(--warning)">Category 04</div>
  <div class="cat-title">Social<br><span style="color:var(--warning)">Engineering</span></div>
  <div class="cat-line" style="background:var(--warning)"></div>
</div>

<div class="cards-grid">

  <!-- PHISHING -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--warning)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🎣</span>
      <span class="card-num">#14</span>
      <div class="card-title">Phishing</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Mass Deception via Fake Communications</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Phishing is a mass attack where criminals send fake emails, texts, or create fake websites impersonating trusted organizations (banks, Amazon, Google) to trick large numbers of people into revealing passwords, credit card numbers, or clicking malicious links.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--warning)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--warning)">📧 Anatomy of a Phishing Email</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">FROM: security@paypa1.com  ← Note: "1" not "l"!
SUBJECT: ⚠️ URGENT: Account Suspended!

"Dear Customer,

Your PayPal account has been SUSPENDED due
to suspicious activity. Click HERE immediately
to verify your identity and restore access.

[CLICK TO VERIFY NOW] ← Links to fake site

Failure to verify within 24 hours will result
in permanent account closure."
                            ← Creates URGENCY!</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-info">
        <span class="alert-icon">🛡️</span>
        <span><strong>Red flags:</strong> Generic greetings, urgent/threatening language, suspicious sender address, hovering over links reveals wrong URL, unexpected requests for credentials.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SPEAR PHISHING -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid #e67e22">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🎯</span>
      <span class="card-num">#15</span>
      <div class="card-title">Spear Phishing</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Targeted Precision Attack</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Unlike generic phishing that targets everyone, spear phishing is a highly targeted attack aimed at a specific individual or organization. The attacker researches their victim first — learning their name, job, colleagues, recent activities — to craft a convincing, personalized message.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:#e67e22">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:#e67e22">🎭 Phishing vs Spear Phishing</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">PHISHING (Casting a wide net):
  "Dear Customer, your account..." 
  Sent to: 100,000 random people
  Success rate: ~1-3%

SPEAR PHISHING (Precise targeting):
  "Hey Sarah, per our meeting with 
   John yesterday about the Q3 budget..."
  Sent to: 1 specific person
  Success rate: ~30-50% 😱</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-warn">
        <span class="alert-icon">🔬</span>
        <span>Attackers use LinkedIn, social media, and company websites to gather information to make the attack believable.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- WHALING -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid #d35400">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🐋</span>
      <span class="card-num">#16</span>
      <div class="card-title">Whaling</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Targeting the Big Fish — C-Suite Executives</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Whaling is spear phishing that targets high-profile, high-value individuals — CEOs, CFOs, directors, and other executives. The attacker goes after the "big whales" because these targets have access to sensitive data, large financial accounts, and decision-making authority.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:#d35400">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:#d35400">🏢 Target Hierarchy</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">       🐋 WHALING
          CEO, CFO, Board Members
        High value = Max damage possible
       
       🎯 SPEAR PHISHING  
        Specific employees, managers
        Targeted but mid-level

      🎣 PHISHING
       Random general public
       Volume-based attack</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-danger">
        <span class="alert-icon">💸</span>
        <span><strong>Business Email Compromise (BEC):</strong> A type of whaling where attackers impersonate the CEO to trick employees into wiring large sums of money. Avg loss: $125,000 per incident.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- SOCIAL ENGINEERING -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🧠</span>
      <span class="card-num">#17</span>
      <div class="card-title">Social Engineering</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Hacking Humans, Not Computers</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Social engineering is the art of psychologically manipulating people into revealing confidential information or performing actions that compromise security. It exploits human psychology — trust, fear, urgency, authority — rather than technical vulnerabilities. The weakest link in security is always the human.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent)">🧪 Psychological Triggers Used</div>
        <table class="compare-table">
          <tbody><tr><th style="color:var(--accent)">Trigger</th><th style="color:var(--accent)">Example</th></tr>
          <tr><td>Authority 👔</td><td>"I'm from IT, I need your password now"</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Urgency ⏰</td><td>"Your account will be deleted in 1 hour!"</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fear 😨</td><td>"You've been hacked, act immediately"</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Greed 💰</td><td>"You won! Click here to claim your prize"</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Trust 🤝</td><td>"Your colleague Mike asked me to contact you"</td></tr>
        </tbody></table>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-good">
        <span class="alert-icon">🛡️</span>
        <span><strong>Defense:</strong> Verify identities independently. Never give passwords verbally. Legitimate IT staff will never ask for your password.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ CATEGORY 5: HACKING & ATTACKS -->
<div class="cat-bar" style="background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--accent2),transparent)"></div>
<section id="hacking">
<div class="category-header">
  <div class="cat-label" style="color:var(--accent2)">Category 05</div>
  <div class="cat-title">Hacking &amp;<br><span style="color:var(--accent2)">Cyber Attacks</span></div>
  <div class="cat-line" style="background:var(--accent2)"></div>
</div>

<div class="cards-grid">

  <!-- HACKING -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid #00b4d8">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">💻</span>
      <span class="card-num">#18</span>
      <div class="card-title">Hacking</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Unauthorized System Access &amp; Manipulation</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Hacking refers to identifying and exploiting weaknesses in computer systems, networks, or applications to gain unauthorized access. The media portrays hackers negatively, but hacking exists on a spectrum from criminal to beneficial — it depends on intent and permission.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:#00b4d8">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:#00b4d8">🎩 The Hat System</div>
        <table class="compare-table">
          <tbody><tr><th style="color:#00b4d8">Hat</th><th style="color:#00b4d8">Type</th><th style="color:#00b4d8">Intent</th></tr>
          <tr><td>⚪ White Hat</td><td>Ethical Hacker</td><td>Authorized, protects systems</td></tr>
          <tr><td>⚫ Black Hat</td><td>Criminal Hacker</td><td>Unauthorized, malicious intent</td></tr>
          <tr><td>🩶 Grey Hat</td><td>In Between</td><td>Unauthorized but reports bugs</td></tr>
          <tr><td>🟢 Green Hat</td><td>Novice/Newbie</td><td>Still learning, may be careless</td></tr>
          <tr><td>🔵 Blue Hat</td><td>Revenge Hacker</td><td>Personal grudge, no deep skills</td></tr>
        </tbody></table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ETHICAL HACKING -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent4)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🛡️</span>
      <span class="card-num">#19</span>
      <div class="card-title">Ethical Hacking</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Authorized Security Testing</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Ethical hacking (also called "white hat hacking") is the practice of legally and intentionally trying to break into systems — with permission — to find vulnerabilities before the bad guys do. Ethical hackers think like attackers but work for defenders.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent4)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent4)">📜 Key Requirements</div>
        <div class="steps">
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(0,255,136,.15);color:var(--accent4)">✓</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Written Permission:</strong> Legal authorization from the organization</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(0,255,136,.15);color:var(--accent4)">✓</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Defined Scope:</strong> Specific systems, time window, allowed techniques</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(0,255,136,.15);color:var(--accent4)">✓</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Report Everything:</strong> All findings documented and reported</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(0,255,136,.15);color:var(--accent4)">✓</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Respect Privacy:</strong> No unauthorized data access beyond scope</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-info">
        <span class="alert-icon">🎓</span>
        <span>Top certifications: <strong>CEH</strong> (Certified Ethical Hacker), <strong>OSCP</strong> (Offensive Security Certified Professional), <strong>CompTIA Security+</strong></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- PENTEST -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent3)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🔍</span>
      <span class="card-num">#20</span>
      <div class="card-title">Penetration Testing</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Pentest — Simulated Cyber Attack</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">A penetration test is a structured, authorized simulation of a cyberattack on a system, network, or application. Companies hire pentesters to try their hardest to break in — exposing real vulnerabilities so they can be fixed before criminals exploit them.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent3)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent3)">📋 The Pentest Methodology</div>
        <div class="steps">
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.15);color:var(--accent3)">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Reconnaissance:</strong> Gather info about target (OSINT, scanning)</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.15);color:var(--accent3)">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Scanning:</strong> Identify open ports, services, OS versions</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.15);color:var(--accent3)">3</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Exploitation:</strong> Attempt to exploit discovered vulnerabilities</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.15);color:var(--accent3)">4</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Post-Exploitation:</strong> Determine impact of successful breach</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.15);color:var(--accent3)">5</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Reporting:</strong> Document all findings and remediation steps</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.1);color:var(--accent3)">Black Box</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.1);color:var(--accent3)">White Box</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.1);color:var(--accent3)">Grey Box</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- VULNERABILITY -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--warning)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🕳️</span>
      <span class="card-num">#21</span>
      <div class="card-title">Vulnerability</div>
      <div class="card-mono">A Weakness That Can Be Exploited</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">A vulnerability is a flaw or weakness in software, hardware, or configuration that can be exploited by an attacker. It's the gap in a system's defenses — think of a crack in a castle wall. Vulnerabilities exist in every system; the goal is to find and fix them first.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--warning)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--warning)">📊 Vulnerability Scoring (CVSS)</div>
        <div class="ascii-art"> CVSS Score  |  Severity
 ────────────────────────
   0.0       |  None
   0.1-3.9   |  Low 🟢
   4.0-6.9   |  Medium 🟡
   7.0-8.9   |  High 🟠
   9.0-10.0  |  CRITICAL 🔴

CVE-2021-44228 (Log4Shell) = 10.0 🔴</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-info">
        <span class="alert-icon">🗂️</span>
        <span>The <strong>CVE database</strong> (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures) publicly tracks and catalogs known vulnerabilities with unique IDs like CVE-2021-44228.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- EXPLOIT -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent2)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">💣</span>
      <span class="card-num">#22</span>
      <div class="card-title">Exploit</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Code That Takes Advantage of a Vulnerability</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">An exploit is a piece of code, script, or technique that takes advantage of a vulnerability to cause unintended behavior — granting access, crashing a system, or escalating privileges. If a vulnerability is the unlocked door, an exploit is knowing how to open it.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent2)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent2)">🔗 Relationship Chain</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">  VULNERABILITY         EXPLOIT
  (The weakness)  +  (The weapon)  =  ATTACK

  Example:
  Buffer overflow  +  Shellcode   =  Root access
  in Apache 2.4       that fills      gained by
  (CVE-2021-xxxx)     the buffer      attacker</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.1);color:var(--accent2)">Local Exploit</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.1);color:var(--accent2)">Remote Exploit</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.1);color:var(--accent2)">Privilege Esc.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ZERO-DAY -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid #e74c3c">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">⚡</span>
      <span class="card-num">#23</span>
      <div class="card-title">Zero-Day</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Unknown Vulnerability — No Patch Exists Yet</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">A zero-day is a previously unknown vulnerability that the software vendor has had "zero days" to fix because they don't know it exists yet. It's the most dangerous type of vulnerability — there's no patch, no defense, and attackers can exploit it freely until discovered.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:#e74c3c">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:#e74c3c">📅 The Zero-Day Timeline</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">Day 0: Vulnerability created (in new code)
         ↓
Day ???: Discovered by researcher or attacker
         ↓
        [Attacker finds it first?]
         ↓              ↓
   EXPLOIT SOLD     [Researcher finds it?]
   on dark web           ↓
   for $$$           Responsible disclosure
                     to vendor → PATCH
                     ← "Zero days" to fix</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-warn">
        <span class="alert-icon">💰</span>
        <span>Zero-day exploits are traded on dark markets. A single iOS zero-day can sell for <strong>$2.5 million+</strong>. Governments also buy them for intelligence operations.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- BOTNET -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid #8e44ad">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🤖</span>
      <span class="card-num">#24</span>
      <div class="card-title">Botnet</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Army of Compromised "Zombie" Computers</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">A botnet is a network of internet-connected devices infected with malware and controlled remotely by a hacker (the "bot herder") without the owners' knowledge. These "zombie" computers can be commanded to send spam, mine cryptocurrency, or launch attacks.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:#8e44ad">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:#8e44ad">🕸️ Botnet Architecture</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">          HACKER
          (Bot Herder)
              |
         [C&amp;C Server]
         (Command &amp;
          Control)
         /    |    \
       Bot   Bot   Bot  ← Your infected PC
       Bot   Bot   Bot     might be here!
       Bot   Bot   Bot
       
  Each bot = a compromised device
  Total bots can reach MILLIONS</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-info">
        <span class="alert-icon">📡</span>
        <span>The Mirai Botnet (2016) infected 600,000+ IoT devices (cameras, routers) and launched the largest DDoS attack ever recorded at the time: 1.2 Tbps.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- DDOS -->
  <div class="card wide-card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent2)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🌊</span>
      <span class="card-num">#25</span>
      <div class="card-title">DDoS Attack</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Distributed Denial of Service — Flooding a Target Offline</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">A DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service) attack overwhelms a target server, network, or service with massive amounts of traffic from many sources simultaneously — making it unavailable to legitimate users. Imagine thousands of people all calling the same phone number at once; the line becomes completely jammed.</p>
      <div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:1rem;">
        <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent2)">
          <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent2)">⚙️ How a DDoS Works</div>
          <div class="steps">
            <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.15);color:var(--accent2)">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Build Botnet:</strong> Attacker infects thousands of devices (bots)</div></div>
            <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.15);color:var(--accent2)">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Command Attack:</strong> Orders all bots to flood the target simultaneously</div></div>
            <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.15);color:var(--accent2)">3</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Overwhelm Server:</strong> Target can't distinguish real users from attack traffic</div></div>
            <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(255,62,108,.15);color:var(--accent2)">4</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Service Down:</strong> Website crashes or becomes too slow to use</div></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--border)">
            <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--muted)">🔠 Attack Types</div>
            <table class="compare-table">
              <tbody><tr><th>Type</th><th>Method</th></tr>
              <tr><td>Volumetric</td><td>Flood with raw traffic (UDP flood)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Protocol</td><td>Exploit server resources (SYN flood)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Application</td><td>Target web app layer (HTTP flood)</td></tr>
            </tbody></table>
          </div>
          <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent4);margin-top:0">
            <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent4)">🛡️ Defenses</div>
            <p>Rate limiting · Traffic scrubbing centers · CDN distribution (Cloudflare) · Anycast network diffusion · Blackhole routing</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ascii-art" style="margin-top:.75rem">
         BOT  BOT  BOT  BOT
          \    |    |   /
    BOT ──▶[ATTACK TRAFFIC]──▶ 🌐 TARGET SERVER 💥 OFFLINE
          /    |    |   \        (Overwhelmed!)
         BOT  BOT  BOT  BOT
         
  Legitimate user: "Why is the website down?" 😠</div>
      <div class="alert alert-warn" style="margin-top:.75rem">
        <span class="alert-icon">📊</span>
        <span>Record DDoS: <strong>3.47 Tbps</strong> attack on Microsoft Azure (2021). GitHub was hit with <strong>1.35 Tbps</strong> in 2018 using memcached amplification. Cost to businesses: $50,000+ per hour of downtime.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- BRUTE FORCE -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid #f39c12">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🔨</span>
      <span class="card-num">#26</span>
      <div class="card-title">Brute Force Attack</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Try Every Possible Password Combination</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">A brute force attack is the simplest form of hacking — it tries every possible combination of characters until the correct password is found. Modern computers can try billions of combinations per second, making short/simple passwords extremely vulnerable.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:#f39c12">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:#f39c12">⏱️ Time to Crack Passwords</div>
        <div class="ascii-art"> Password    | Strength  | Time to Crack
 ─────────────────────────────────────
 "1234"      | 😱 None   | Instantly
 "password"  | 😱 None   | Instantly  
 "abc123"    | 🔴 Weak   | &lt; 1 second
 "Summer2024"| 🟡 Medium | 3 hours
 "X#9kL!mP2" | 🟢 Strong | 14 years
 "correct-horse-battery-staple"
             | 🟢 Strong | Centuries!</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-good">
        <span class="alert-icon">💡</span>
        <span><strong>Dictionary attack:</strong> Instead of random characters, tries common words and known passwords. <strong>Credential stuffing:</strong> Uses leaked username/password pairs from data breaches.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>
</section>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════ CATEGORY 6: DEFENSE & FORENSICS -->
<div class="cat-bar" style="background:linear-gradient(90deg,var(--accent4),transparent)"></div>
<section id="defense">
<div class="category-header">
  <div class="cat-label" style="color:var(--accent4)">Category 06</div>
  <div class="cat-title">Defense &amp;<br><span style="color:var(--accent4)">Digital Forensics</span></div>
  <div class="cat-line" style="background:var(--accent4)"></div>
</div>

<div class="cards-grid">

  <!-- ENCRYPTION -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🔐</span>
      <span class="card-num">#27</span>
      <div class="card-title">Encryption</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Scrambling Data So Only Authorized Parties Can Read It</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Encryption is the process of converting readable data (plaintext) into an unreadable scrambled format (ciphertext) using a mathematical algorithm and a key. Only someone with the correct decryption key can reverse the process and read the original data.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent)">🔑 Symmetric vs Asymmetric</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">SYMMETRIC (Same key for both):
  Alice ──[🔑Key]──▶ encrypt ──▶ 🔒cipher
  Bob   ──[🔑Key]──▶ decrypt ──▶ 📄plaintext
  Problem: How to share the key safely?

ASYMMETRIC (Public/Private key pair):
  Bob's PUBLIC key  = anyone can use to LOCK
  Bob's PRIVATE key = only Bob can UNLOCK
  
  Alice ──[Bob's Public Key]──▶ 🔒cipher
  Bob   ──[Bob's Private Key]──▶ 📄plaintext</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-info">
        <span class="alert-icon">🌐</span>
        <span>Every time you see <strong>HTTPS</strong> in a URL, your data is encrypted using TLS (Transport Layer Security). The padlock icon = your data is safe in transit.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="tag-row">
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(0,229,255,.1);color:var(--accent)">AES-256</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(0,229,255,.1);color:var(--accent)">RSA</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(0,229,255,.1);color:var(--accent)">TLS/SSL</span>
        <span class="tag" style="background:rgba(0,229,255,.1);color:var(--accent)">End-to-End</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- DECRYPTION -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent4)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🔓</span>
      <span class="card-num">#28</span>
      <div class="card-title">Decryption</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Reversing Encryption to Recover Plaintext</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Decryption is the reverse process of encryption — converting scrambled ciphertext back into readable plaintext using the correct key. Without the key, modern encryption is computationally infeasible to break (it would take longer than the age of the universe).</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent4)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent4)">🔄 The Full Cycle</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">Original:  "Hello, World!"
              ↓ ENCRYPT (AES-256 + Key)
Ciphertext: "7f4a9c2b1e8d..." (unreadable)
              ↓ TRANSMIT safely over internet
Received:  "7f4a9c2b1e8d..."
              ↓ DECRYPT (same/paired Key)
Recovered: "Hello, World!" ✓</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-warn">
        <span class="alert-icon">⚠️</span>
        <span><strong>Key management is critical.</strong> Encryption is only as strong as the security of your keys. A leaked private key means all encrypted data is compromised.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- DIGITAL FORENSICS -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--accent3)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">🔬</span>
      <span class="card-num">#29</span>
      <div class="card-title">Digital Forensics</div>
      <div class="card-mono">Investigating Cybercrime Like a Digital Detective</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Digital forensics is the science of collecting, preserving, analyzing, and presenting digital evidence from computers, phones, networks, and cloud systems for use in legal investigations. It answers: What happened? Who did it? When? How?</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--accent3)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--accent3)">🕵️ Forensic Investigation Steps</div>
        <div class="steps">
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.15);color:var(--accent3)">1</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Identification:</strong> Identify potential digital evidence sources</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.15);color:var(--accent3)">2</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Preservation:</strong> Create exact forensic copies (bit-by-bit images)</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.15);color:var(--accent3)">3</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Analysis:</strong> Examine files, logs, metadata, deleted data, browser history</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.15);color:var(--accent3)">4</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Documentation:</strong> Record all findings with timestamps</div></div>
          <div class="step"><div class="step-num" style="background:rgba(162,89,255,.15);color:var(--accent3)">5</div><div class="step-content"><strong>Presentation:</strong> Present evidence clearly in court or report</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-info">
        <span class="alert-icon">🛠️</span>
        <span>Key tools: <strong>Autopsy</strong> (disk analysis), <strong>Wireshark</strong> (network capture), <strong>Volatility</strong> (memory forensics), <strong>FTK</strong> (Forensic Toolkit)</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- CHAIN OF CUSTODY -->
  <div class="card" style="border-top:3px solid var(--warning)">
    <div class="card-top">
      <span class="card-icon">⛓️</span>
      <span class="card-num">#30</span>
      <div class="card-title">Chain of Custody</div>
      <div class="card-mono">The Legal Record of Evidence Handling</div>
    </div>
    <div class="card-body">
      <p class="card-desc">Chain of custody is the documented, chronological record of who collected, handled, transferred, and analyzed digital evidence — from the moment it was discovered to its presentation in court. It proves the evidence was not tampered with or contaminated.</p>
      <div class="card-box" style="border-color:var(--warning)">
        <div class="card-box-title" style="color:var(--warning)">📋 Chain of Custody Log</div>
        <div class="ascii-art">ITEM: Laptop (Serial: HP-X372-9K)
CASE: CYB-2025-0047

Date/Time  | Handler        | Action
────────────────────────────────────
2025-03-14 | Inv. Martinez  | Seized at scene
09:15 AM   | (Badge #1234)  | Photographed in place
           |                |
2025-03-14 | Inv. Martinez  | Transferred to lab
02:30 PM   | → Forensic Kim | Signed over, sealed bag
           |                |
2025-03-15 | Forensic Kim   | Forensic image made
10:00 AM   | (Badge #5678)  | Hash verified: SHA-256</div>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-danger">
        <span class="alert-icon">⚖️</span>
        <span><strong>Breaking the chain</strong> (gaps in documentation, missing signatures, evidence left unattended) can make evidence <strong>inadmissible in court</strong> — letting criminals go free.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="alert alert-good">
        <span class="alert-icon">🔒</span>
        <span><strong>Hash verification:</strong> After making a forensic copy, investigators compare cryptographic hashes (SHA-256) of original and copy. If they match exactly, the copy is proven unaltered.</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>
</section>

